        <div id="what-matters" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. What Actually Makes a Good Deck Stain Brush
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">A Stain Brush Is Not a Paint Brush</h3>

          <p>
            A deck stain brush has a different job from a paint brush. A paint brush is meant to lay a film on top of a surface and leave it smooth. A deck stain brush is meant to push a thin liquid into rough, porous wood, work it into the grain and the checks, and drag away whatever the wood will not absorb. That changes what matters. Holding capacity and stiffness matter far more than a fine tip, and a crisp cutting edge matters hardly at all, because there is nothing on a deck floor to cut in against.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Shape, Bristle and Pole Thread</h3>

          <p>
            Three things decide whether a brush is right for the job. The first is shape. A block brush, a thick square head on a short handle, carries a lot of stain and presses it into flat boards. An angled sash brush is for edges and railings. A flat wall brush is in between. The second is bristle. Natural bristle suits oil-based stain; synthetic bristle suits water-based stain, and using the wrong one is the most common way a decent brush turns into a bad one halfway through the job. The third is whether the handle threads onto an extension pole, because a brush you have to use on your knees across a 300 square foot floor is a brush you will use badly by the second hour.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Width</h3>

          <p>
            Width matters less than people think, but it still matters. Most decking is 5-1/2 inches wide. A 4-inch brush covers most of a board's face in one stroke and still fits between a board and the house wall. A brush wider than the board just wastes stain into the gaps on either side. Anything narrower than 3 inches is a railing brush, not a floor brush.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Back-Brushing Tool or Only Applicator</h3>

          <p>
            It also helps to be honest about what the brush is for. If you are applying stain with a pad, roller or sprayer, the brush is the back-brushing tool, and it can be cheaper and simpler. If the brush is the only applicator, which is reasonable on a small deck, it needs to hold more stain and go on a pole. Our <a href="/brush-vs-roller-vs-sprayer-deck-stain/">brush vs roller vs sprayer comparison</a> covers which applicator to use on each surface; this guide is about which brushes and pads are worth buying once you have decided.
          </p>

