        <div id="block-vs-angled" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Block Brushes vs Angled Brushes vs Flat Brushes
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Block Brushes</h3>

          <p>
            The block brush is the one most people have never bought. It looks like a scrubbing brush with soft bristles: a thick, square-cut head, often 4 to 5-1/2 inches wide and well over an inch deep, mounted on a short handle that may or may not thread onto a pole. The depth is the point. It holds a large reservoir of thin stain and releases it slowly as you drag it along a board, so one dip can cover several feet of decking. Pressing down drives the stain into the grain and into the shallow checks that open up on any deck more than a year or two old. The Wooster Bravo Stainer is the obvious example.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Angled Sash Brushes</h3>

          <p>
            The angled sash brush is the opposite. It is thin, tapered and designed to cut a clean line along trim. On a deck that is useful in exactly one place: where the deck stain meets something it should not touch, such as house siding, a painted post or the underside of a door threshold. Anywhere else an angled brush is slow, carries too little stain, and gets loaded too heavily at the tip, which leaves drips on the vertical faces of balusters. If you already own a good angled sash from painting interior trim, use it for those few edge lines and nowhere else.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Flat Brushes</h3>

          <p>
            Flat brushes sit in between. A 3 to 4-inch flat wall brush carries more stain than a sash brush and gives a smoother film on vertical surfaces than a block brush, which is why it is the right choice for railings, fascia boards, stair stringers and anything people will look at up close. A flat brush is also what you want for solid and semi-solid stains, which behave more like thin paint and look streaky if they are scrubbed in with a block brush. If you are applying a solid color, our <a href="/best-solid-deck-stain/">solid deck stain guide</a> explains why those products need a different approach from the penetrating stains a block brush is built for.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">One Floor Tool, One Railing Tool</h3>

          <p>
            In practice, a typical deck needs one floor tool and one railing tool. For an oil-based penetrating stain that means a block brush and a 3-inch natural bristle flat brush. For a water-based or solid stain it means a pad or polyester flat brush on the floor and a synthetic flat brush on the rails. Buying a whole set of brushes in different widths is rarely worth it.
          </p>

